'''''Ficus callosa''''' is a species of tree in the family [[Moraceae]] native to India, southern China, [[Indo-China]] and [[Malesia]].{{R|POWO}} In Vietnam it may be called ''đa chai''<ref name = "CCVN"/> or ''đa gùa''.

==Description==
''Ficus callosa'' is a tree up to about {{cvt|35-45|m}} tall with a trunk to about {{cvt|35|cm}} diameter, and it may have large [[Buttress root|buttresses]]. The leaves of young plants are lobed and larger than those on older plants. They are shiny green above and paler below, with about 8–11 pairs of lateral veins. The [[syconium|syconia]] (fig fruit) are produced in the {{botanygloss|axil|leaf axils}} either singly or in pairs, and are green-yellow when mature; they are somewhat pear-shaped and measure about {{cvt|25|mm|0}} long and {{cvt|15|mm|1}} wide. The species is [[Dioecy|dioecious]], with female, male and gall flowers all present in the syconium.{{R|FOC|FL-MALESIANA|TFOB}}

